1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:05,846
This video shows you how to get a certificate from Let's Encrypt,

2
00:00:05,846 --> 00:00:09,070
so your browser trusts your leader with no setup.

3
00:00:09,120 --> 00:00:13,900
Your leader runs its own worker, which reaches it at 127.0.0.1.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:18,224
Let's Encrypt won't issue a certificate for that address,

5
00:00:18,224 --> 00:00:22,150
and the leader refuses one that doesn't cover it.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,683
So tell that worker to use your leader's name.

7
00:00:25,683 --> 00:00:29,830
Add SMELTR_LEADER_URL, set to your leader's address.

8
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,405
Then restart the leader.

9
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:39,270
Chrome warns, because your leader signed its own certificate.

10
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:43,222
Go past the warning one last time, and sign in.

11
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:53,380
In Admin, open System, then Leader certificate.

12
00:00:53,380 --> 00:00:56,315
Under Source, choose ACME.

13
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,641
The directory is Let's Encrypt.

14
00:01:02,641 --> 00:01:08,110
For another authority, choose Other, and enter its directory URL.

15
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,750
Names holds your leader's name.

16
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:14,952
Enter a contact email, and agree to the terms of service.

17
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:22,269
Under Advanced, Challenge picks how Let's Encrypt checks that the name is yours.

18
00:01:22,269 --> 00:01:27,750
With TLS-ALPN-01, port 443 on the name must reach the leader.

19
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:31,190
With HTTP-01, port 80 must reach it.

20
00:01:31,240 --> 00:01:34,748
With DNS-01, the leader proves it with a DNS record,

21
00:01:34,748 --> 00:01:38,510
so nothing has to reach the leader from the internet.

22
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,974
The leader publishes that record through Cloudflare.

23
00:01:41,974 --> 00:01:46,670
Paste an API token that can read your zone and edit its DNS records.

24
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:48,990
Select Get certificate.

25
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:50,720
The page shows each step.

26
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:53,673
The leader adds the DNS record in Cloudflare,

27
00:01:53,673 --> 00:01:57,488
and waits for the zone's nameservers to serve it.

28
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:07,830
Then Let's Encrypt checks the names, and the leader installs the certificate.

29
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:13,210
It comes from a new authority, so the leader hands that authority to your workers first.

30
00:02:13,210 --> 00:02:17,230
Once they all hold it, the leader serves the new certificate.

31
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:19,563
The leader renews it on its own.

32
00:02:19,563 --> 00:02:21,230
The page shows when.

33
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,910
Open smeltr in a new browser window.

34
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:27,608
There's no warning this time, and nothing to install.

35
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,218
Check on learning.

36
00:02:30,418 --> 00:02:33,258
Your leader is on a private network.

37
00:02:33,258 --> 00:02:37,565
Why can it still get a certificate with DNS-01?

38
00:02:41,965 --> 00:02:46,321
Let's Encrypt checks a DNS record in your domain, not the leader.

39
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:49,065
Nothing has to reach the leader.

40
00:02:49,965 --> 00:02:54,039
Next, you put a reverse proxy in front of your leader.
